{
  "question": "A 5-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of abdominal pain, vomiting, and diarrhea for 6 days. Her mother says that over the last 24 hours she has developed a rash and has been urinating less frequently than usual. One month ago, she had a 3-day episode of high fever and sore throat that subsided without medical treatment. She appears weak. Her temperature is 37.7°C (99.8°F), pulse is 120/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 114/72 mm Hg. Examination shows petechiae on the trunk and jaundice of the skin. The abdomen is diffusely tender with no peritoneal signs. Neurological examination shows no abnormalities. Laboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 8 g/dL\nMean corpuscular volume 85 μm3\nLeukocyte count 16,200/mm3\nPlatelet count 38,000/mm3\nSerum\nBlood urea nitrogen 43 mg/dL\nCreatinine 2.9 mg/dL\npH 7.0\nUrine dipstick is positive for blood and protein. A blood smear shows schistocytes and normochromic, normocytic cells. In addition to supportive treatment, which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management of this patient?\"?",
  "is_multi_choice": true,
